Perfect! Now I can see the check at lines 481-487. The issue is clear:

```rust
if dotted && !sweet_child_of_mine.is_implicit() {
    let key_span = get_key_span(key).expect("all keys have spans");
    errors.report_error(
        ParseError::new("duplicate key").with_unexpected(key_span),
    );
    return None;
}
```

This check prevents dotted keys from extending ANY non-implicit table. But according to the TOML spec and the issue, we should allow dotted keys to extend tables that were implicitly created, even if their parent table later became explicit.

The fix is to also check whether the table was explicitly defined with its own `[table]` header. Looking at the code, `set_position` is called in `start_table`, so we can check if `position` is set to determine if the table had an explicit header.

